// ─── SECTION 1 — Problem Description ─────────────────────────────────────────
function section1() {
  return [
    h1("1. Problem Description"),
    body("Retail demand forecasting is a fundamental challenge in supply chain management. Accurate weekly sales predictions enable retailers to optimize inventory levels, allocate staffing resources, plan promotional campaigns, and minimize both overstock and stockout scenarios. Walmart, as one of the world's largest retailers, operates thousands of stores across the United States, each subject to unique local conditions including regional economic factors, seasonal trends, and promotional activity."),
    body("This project addresses the problem of predicting weekly department-level sales for Walmart stores using historical transactional data combined with external macroeconomic and store-level features. The forecasting task is formulated as a supervised regression problem, where the model learns from past observations to predict future sales figures."),
    body("Key challenges inherent to this problem include:"),
    bullet("High dimensionality introduced by one-hot encoding of store, department, and store-type identifiers, resulting in over 130 features."),
    bullet("Significant sparsity in promotional markdown data — approximately 64% of entries carry a zero markdown value, reflecting the pre-November 2011 period before Walmart launched broad promotional programs."),
    bullet("Non-linear relationships between economic indicators (CPI, unemployment, fuel price, temperature) and weekly sales that simple linear models cannot capture adequately."),
    bullet("Temporal dependency in the data, necessitating a chronological train/test split to prevent data leakage from future weeks into the training set."),
    spacer(120),
    body("To address these challenges, this project implements four machine learning models organized within a dual-pipeline architecture: a Normalized Random Forest, an Unnormalized Random Forest, a Linear Regression baseline, and an XGBoost gradient-boosted ensemble. The system is deployed as an interactive Streamlit web application, enabling real-time sales prediction through a user-facing interface."),
  ];
}

// ─── SECTION 2 — Data Preparation ────────────────────────────────────────────
function section2() {
  return [
    h1("2. Data Preparation"),
    h2("2.1 Dataset Description"),
    body("The Walmart Store Sales Forecasting dataset is sourced from the Kaggle competition of the same name. It consists of four inter-related files covering 45 stores over a period from February 2010 to October 2012 (approximately 143 weeks)."),
    spacer(60),
    metricsTable(
      ["File", "Format", "Shape", "Description"],
      [
        ["train.csv", "CSV", "421,570 × 5", "Weekly sales per store-department pair with holiday flag"],
        ["features.xlsx", "Excel", "8,190 × 12", "Store-level economic and promotional features per week"],
        ["stores.csv", "CSV", "45 × 3", "Store identifiers, type classification (A/B/C), and physical size"],
        ["test.csv", "CSV", "115,064 × 4", "Hold-out prediction set (no Weekly_Sales column)"],
      ]
    ),
    spacer(120),
    h2("2.2 Feature Inventory"),
    body("After merging all four files on shared keys (Store, Date, IsHoliday), the consolidated full_df dataset contains the following features:"),
    spacer(60),
    metricsTable(
      ["Feature", "Type", "Description"],
      [
        ["Store", "Integer (OHE)", "Store identifier (1–45), one-hot encoded"],
        ["Dept", "Integer (OHE)", "Department identifier (1–99), one-hot encoded"],
        ["Date", "Datetime", "Week ending date — used for chronological split, then dropped"],
        ["IsHoliday", "Binary (int)", "1 if the week contains a major US holiday, 0 otherwise"],
        ["Temperature", "Continuous (°F)", "Average regional temperature for that week"],
        ["Fuel_Price", "Continuous ($)", "Regional average fuel price per gallon"],
        ["CPI", "Continuous", "Consumer Price Index — measure of purchasing power"],
        ["Unemployment", "Continuous (%)", "Regional unemployment rate"],
        ["MarkDown", "Continuous ($)", "Aggregated sum of five anonymized markdown columns (MD1–MD5)"],
        ["Type", "Categorical (OHE)", "Store type: A (largest), B, or C (smallest)"],
        ["Size", "Continuous (sq ft)", "Physical store floor area — structural feature left on raw scale"],
        ["Weekly_Sales", "Target ($)", "Aggregate weekly sales for a given store-department pair"],
      ]
    ),
    spacer(120),
    h2("2.3 Data Cleaning"),
    body("The cleaning pipeline proceeded through the following steps:"),
    numbered("Date parsing: Date columns across all three files were cast to pandas datetime objects to enable chronological sorting and temporal feature analysis."),
    numbered("Null handling: MarkDown columns MD1–MD5 exhibited up to 65% missing values, reflecting the absence of promotional activity in the pre-November 2011 period. All five columns were imputed with zero (reflecting no markdown), clipped at zero to remove negative entries, and then summed into a single aggregated MarkDown feature."),
    numbered("Boolean encoding: The IsHoliday flag was cast from boolean to integer (0/1) for compatibility with scikit-learn estimators."),
    numbered("Dataset merge: stores_df was left-joined onto features_df on the Store key to preserve the Size column, which is critical for model accuracy. The resulting features frame was then joined to train_df on [Store, Date, IsHoliday]."),
    numbered("Outlier capping: An IQR-based capping procedure was applied to four continuous economic features — Temperature, Fuel_Price, CPI, and Unemployment — prior to normalization. Values outside the [Q1 − 1.5·IQR, Q3 + 1.5·IQR] interval were clipped to the boundary, reducing the influence of sensor anomalies and data recording errors."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("2.4 Feature Engineering"),
    body("The five individual markdown columns were transformed into a single composite MarkDown feature:"),
    bullet("Fill NaN → 0 (no markdown if unreported)"),
    bullet("Clip negative values to 0 (data quality correction)"),
    bullet("Sum all five: MarkDown = MD1 + MD2 + MD3 + MD4 + MD5"),
    spacer(80),
    body("One-hot encoding was applied to the categorical columns Store, Dept, and Type using pandas get_dummies with drop_first=True to avoid multicollinearity. This expanded the feature matrix to 133 columns. Residual NaN values after encoding were imputed with the column median."),
  ];
}

// ─── SECTION 3 — Methods and Models ──────────────────────────────────────────
function section3() {
  return [
    h1("3. Methods and Models"),
    h2("3.1 Overall Architecture"),
    body("The project implements a dual-pipeline architecture that trains the same set of models twice — once with MinMax normalization applied to numeric features and once without — enabling a rigorous empirical comparison of normalization impact on tree-based ensembles versus linear models."),
    spacer(60),
    metricsTable(
      ["Pipeline", "Normalization", "Models Applied", "Saved Artifacts"],
      [
        ["Approach 1 — Normalized", "MinMaxScaler on 5 numeric features", "RF, Linear Regression", "model_norm.pkl, scaler.pkl"],
        ["Approach 2 — Unnormalized", "None — raw feature scales", "RF, XGBoost", "model_raw.pkl"],
      ]
    ),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.2 Chronological Train/Test Split"),
    body("A chronological 80/20 split was applied to prevent temporal data leakage. All rows were sorted by Date ascending; the first 80% formed the training set and the final 20% formed the test set. No random shuffling was performed."),
    spacer(60),
    metricsTable(
      ["Split", "Row Count", "Date Range"],
      [
        ["Training set", "337,256 rows", "2010-02-05 → 2012-04-13"],
        ["Test set", "84,314 rows", "2012-04-13 → 2012-10-26"],
      ]
    ),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.3 Normalization Strategy"),
    body("MinMaxScaler was fit exclusively on training rows and applied via .transform() on both train and test sets. This prevents data leakage: the scaler has no knowledge of the test distribution at fit time. The five continuous economic/promotional features were normalized: Temperature, Fuel_Price, CPI, Unemployment, and MarkDown."),
    body("The Size feature was deliberately excluded from scaling because Random Forest uses rank-order split comparisons, making it invariant to absolute scale. Maintaining Size on its raw scale (34,875–219,622 sq ft) preserves the tree's interpretability for that feature."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.4 Random Forest Regressor"),
    body("Random Forest is a tree-based ensemble that trains n_estimators independent decision trees on bootstrap samples of the training data (bagging) and averages their predictions. Each split considers a random subset of features (sqrt(n_features) by default for classification; n_features/3 for regression), reducing inter-tree correlation and improving generalization."),
    mixed(bold("Key parameters: "), normal("n_estimators=100, random_state=42, n_jobs=−1 (all cores). Training time: ~310–340 seconds per pipeline.")),
    body("Because Random Forest partitions feature space using rank-order thresholds rather than Euclidean distance metrics, it is theoretically invariant to monotonic feature transformations including normalization. This project empirically verifies this property by training both a normalized and unnormalized Random Forest and comparing results."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.5 Linear Regression"),
    body("Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Linear Regression minimizes the sum of squared residuals between predicted and actual values. It assumes a linear relationship between the feature matrix X and the target y, expressed as:"),
    new Paragraph({
      alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
      spacing: { before: 80, after: 80 },
      children: [new TextRun({ text: "ŷ = β₀ + β₁x₁ + β₂x₂ + … + βₙxₙ", italics: true, size: 22, font: "Arial" })]
    }),
    body("Unlike tree-based methods, Linear Regression is scale-sensitive: features with large numeric ranges dominate the coefficient estimation. The normalized feature set (X_train_norm) was therefore reused directly for the Linear Regression pipeline, requiring no additional preprocessing."),
    mixed(bold("Training time: "), normal("approximately 5.65 seconds — significantly faster than ensemble methods.")),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.6 XGBoost Regressor"),
    body("XGBoost (eXtreme Gradient Boosting) is a gradient-boosted decision tree ensemble. Unlike Random Forest, which trains trees in parallel on bootstrap samples, XGBoost trains trees sequentially — each tree corrects the residual errors of the cumulative ensemble. The objective function combines a differentiable loss term with an explicit regularization term:"),
    new Paragraph({
      alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
      spacing: { before: 80, after: 80 },
      children: [new TextRun({ text: "L(φ) = Σᵢ l(ŷᵢ, yᵢ) + Σₖ Ω(fₖ)   where  Ω(f) = γT + ½λ‖w‖²", italics: true, size: 22, font: "Arial" })]
    }),
    body("XGBoost is scale-invariant by the same rank-order argument as Random Forest; the unnormalized feature set (X_train_raw) was therefore used directly."),
    spacer(60),
    metricsTable(
      ["Hyperparameter", "Value", "Rationale"],
      [
        ["n_estimators", "300", "More trees than RF to compensate for lower per-tree strength"],
        ["learning_rate", "0.1", "Moderate shrinkage — balances speed and generalization"],
        ["max_depth", "6", "Slightly shallower than RF default to reduce overfitting"],
        ["subsample", "0.8", "80% row sampling per tree — stochastic gradient boosting"],
        ["colsample_bytree", "0.8", "80% column sampling per tree"],
        ["random_state", "42", "Reproducibility"],
      ]
    ),
    mixed(bold("Training time: "), normal("approximately 13.6 seconds — significantly faster than Random Forest due to more efficient tree construction.")),
    spacer(120),
    h2("3.7 Streamlit Deployment"),
    body("Both the normalized and unnormalized Random Forest pipelines, along with Linear Regression and XGBoost, were serialized using joblib and deployed in a Streamlit web application. The app allows users to select a prediction pipeline, input store and department identifiers, adjust economic and promotional sliders, and receive an instant weekly sales forecast. The MarkDown slider range was set to $0–$200,000 to cover the full training distribution (max = $160,510.61), ensuring model responsiveness across the complete input domain."),
  ];
}

