}

// ─── SECTION 5 — Discussion ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
function section5() {
  return [
    h1("5. Discussion"),
    h2("5.1 Model Performance Analysis"),
    body("The experimental results reveal a clear performance hierarchy across the four models:"),
    body("Random Forest (both pipelines) achieves the strongest test performance, with an R² of approximately 0.942 and MAE under $2,654. The minimal gap between training and test R² (0.0494–0.0496) indicates well-controlled overfitting, attributable to the ensemble's inherent variance-reduction mechanism and the large, diverse training set."),
    body("XGBoost occupies a middle position with R² = 0.920 and MAE of $3,908. While XGBoost typically outperforms Random Forest on tabular datasets, the opposite occurs here due to dataset characteristics: the feature matrix is dominated by high-cardinality one-hot encodings of stores and departments. Random Forest's parallel bagging with per-tree column subsampling handles this sparse, high-dimensional space more effectively than XGBoost's sequential boosting. Additionally, XGBoost's training time (13.6s) is significantly faster than Random Forest (~310s), representing a favorable efficiency-accuracy trade-off for production deployment."),
    body("Linear Regression performs substantially worse (R² = 0.695, MAE = $7,982), confirming that the weekly sales prediction task has significant non-linear structure that a linear model cannot capture. The residual analysis shows a fan-shaped pattern (heteroscedasticity) and structured Q-Q plot deviations, both indicating systematic violation of the OLS assumptions of linearity and homoscedasticity in this dataset."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("5.2 Normalization and Tree-Based Models"),
    body("A central finding of this study is the empirical confirmation that normalization has essentially zero impact on Random Forest performance: the MAE difference is $0.54, R² difference is 0.0003, and train/test overfit gap difference is 0.0002. This aligns with the theoretical expectation — decision tree splits are determined by the best threshold for a single feature at a time, and MinMax scaling preserves rank order, leaving all split boundaries mathematically equivalent."),
    body("This finding has practical implications: for Random Forest (and gradient-boosted tree variants), the engineering overhead of fitting, saving, versioning, and applying a MinMax scaler provides no accuracy benefit. The normalized pipeline was retained in deployment because it enables Linear Regression to run on the same preprocessed feature set without a separate preprocessing chain."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("5.3 Feature Importance Insights"),
    body("Across all non-linear models, the feature importance analysis consistently identifies Store Size as the single most important predictor (~18.9%), followed by high-volume department dummies (Dept_92, Dept_95, Dept_38). This reflects the fundamental retail intuition that a store's physical footprint — a proxy for product range and customer capacity — largely determines its sales potential, and that certain departments (e.g., grocery, electronics) generate disproportionately high weekly revenue."),
    body("MarkDown ranks #19 of 133 features at ~1.09% importance, despite being a designated promotional metric. This low ranking is explained by the sparsity analysis: 64.1% of MarkDown values are zero (representing all pre-November 2011 data). The tree splits on the dominant Store and Dept identifiers early in each tree, leaving MarkDown as a marginal refinement signal. However, post-2011 data shows a Pearson correlation of +0.123 with weekly sales, confirming that MarkDown does carry a real signal — it simply cannot compete with the stronger identity-based signals in this dataset."),
    spacer(120),
    h2("5.4 Deployment Considerations"),
    body("The Streamlit application exposes both normalized and unnormalized pipelines through a unified interface. Key deployment decisions include:"),
    bullet("Slider range for MarkDown: Set to $0–$200,000 to cover the full training maximum of $160,510.61. A slider capped at $5,000 (tested in earlier versions) represents only 3.1% of the training range and produces near-zero model response — a subtle but critical UX bug."),
    bullet("Separate scaler.pkl: The MinMax scaler fitted in the normalized pipeline is shared between the Normalized RF and Linear Regression prediction paths, reducing file redundancy."),
    bullet("XGBoost does not require a scaler at prediction time; its prediction path uses raw feature values directly."),
  ];
}

